1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
And now, the old scout master!

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Did you guys hear my dog?

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Yes

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
She's a foxhound and if something walks by the house like within a half a mile of the house,

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
she gets very interested and needs to let me know

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
I think I've heard her before

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
So that's going to be edited out, huh?

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Well, we'll see if it turns out to be kind of interesting and funny, we'll leave it

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Hey, this is podcast number 88

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Hey, welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast, this is Clark Green

11
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Peter LaRue wrote in about last week's interview with Mark Dyer and Doug Metz about Shelter Box

12
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
And if you haven't heard that one, you know, make sure you get a listen to it

13
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Peter wrote in and said that he saw Shelter Box at the World Jamboree

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
and he's been very keen to investigate introducing it to scouting in South Africa

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
So we're glad we could help make that connection

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Also from Tom Gillard, and you're going to hear from Tom later on

17
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
He said, we had our full district camperee this past weekend

18
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Some of the other adults and I have been talking about the respectful distance we need to establish from scouts

19
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:55,000
I informed the committee at the last meeting in the intent of moving the adult camp about 150 feet down the road from the troop at the camperee

20
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,000
I informed the senior patrol leader of this on the ride out there

21
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
And frankly, he was a little shocked and I think maybe a little bit scared

22
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:11,000
But everything went very well, mostly because we adults couldn't see all the small things that we might interfere with

23
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
A few times I went down to the scout campsite, I stopped at the entrance and I asked permission to enter

24
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
And that kind of shocked most of them

25
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
I would ask the senior patrol leader if he had the schedule

26
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Tell them to stop by and pick us up on the way to activities and they did without any clock checking on my part

27
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Now they didn't get there as early as I would have, but they really weren't very late either

28
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,000
I gave the senior patrol leader a safety review and basically we left him alone

29
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
And that turned that weekend into a relaxing one for me and the other adults

30
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Not that I wasn't looking through the woods to see how large the fire was and things like that

31
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,000
But the adults could sit around and talk about stuff and just be a little more relaxed

32
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
After lunch I stopped in to see how things were going with the scouts

33
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
They had done all the activities and weren't going back out

34
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
I asked them what they were going to do with themselves that afternoon

35
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
They said, well, we're going to do some advancement with the younger scouts

36
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
I almost had to sit down

37
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Anyway, they had a good time

38
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Sunday morning I was asking the scouts what they would be telling their fellow scouts

39
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
That they didn't make the camperee

40
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Would they tell them what went on? What did they think of it?

41
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
I emphasized that it was a trusted responsibility

42
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
And I think they really understood this because they wanted to continue

43
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
I've been kind of reluctant to try this step of being a little bit more at arm's length from them

44
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
And I've been reluctant for quite a while

45
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
But this experience reinforced what you've written about on the blog

46
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
And talked about on the podcast and, of course, what Baden Powell had to say

47
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
That's all for now and that's Tom Gillard in Tollahoma, Tennessee

48
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
And that's a great report

49
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,000
It's a challenge to let scouts do things for themselves sometimes

50
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
It's a constant challenge for me, I know it is for most of you

51
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,000
But when we do let them do things for themselves

52
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
That's when the program really starts to click, isn't it?

53
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
I mean, that's when it really kicks in

54
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Let me take note too that there is a new Eagle Project booklet

55
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
That has just been released within the past week

56
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
And a new advancement guide that's just been released

57
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
These are two pretty key pieces of knowledge for every scout leader to have

58
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,000
And we'll be discussing them on the blog and in the podcast in the coming weeks

59
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Because some of the changes are very encouraging indeed

60
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
There are some things that have changed

61
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
And that we will need to adjust some of our procedures

62
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
To recognize those changes

63
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
And there are some things that have changed

64
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
That are an emphasis of what was there before

65
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
But they're just spelled out a bit clearly

66
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,000
And I have taken the time to review both of those documents

67
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:51,000
I think it's fantastic

68
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
I think it's going to make a whole lot of difference

69
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Especially in those kind of disputed, troubled, eagle things that happen

70
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
And, you know, boards of review and scout master conferences

71
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Kind of sets a clearer path for how to resolve conflicts

72
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
What both of these new documents do

73
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Is emphasize the importance of reaching the scout with the program

74
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
And to make sure that our policies and procedures are designed with that in mind

75
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
So next up, and for the rest of the podcast is our first scout master panel

76
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,000
That's right, we've got a panel of scout masters put together

77
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
From all over the country

78
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
There were three of us this time

79
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
And I'm very interested to see what direction this takes

80
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
I think we've got a pretty solid thing put together

81
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
And I think you'll enjoy listening

82
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
This time around our question for the panel was

83
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
What would you tell a new scout master?

84
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:47,000
What would you tell somebody who's considering taking the job?

85
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:48,000
And I think you'll enjoy it

86
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
And that's going to take up the remainder of the podcast

87
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
So let's get started, shall we?

88
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
And everything that feels just as it should

89
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
You're part of a life there

90
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
You're part of something good

91
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:16,000
If you've ever wandered lonely through the woods

92
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,000
If you'd ever been out walking new and known

93
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
So on the scout masters podcast today

94
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,000
We're trying a little experiment

95
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
We've got a panel of scout masters put together

96
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
And we're going to talk about

97
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
After we've been through this for several years

98
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
How we would advise a new scout master

99
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
What we would tell him that we know now

100
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
That we didn't know then

101
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
From Vera, Florida

102
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Larry Geiger is joining me

103
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And Larry has been a scout master

104
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
For several different troops over the years

105
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
And is now an assistant scout master

106
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
For the troop he was most recently scout master

107
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,000
For troops 720 there in Vera

108
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:03,000
How you doing today, Larry?

109
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Good, doing good

110
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Tom Gillard is in tele home of Tennessee

111
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
Where he is the scout master of troop 402

112
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Welcome to the podcast, Tom

113
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Thank you very much

114
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,000
And Walter Underwood is a past scout master

115
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,000
Of troop 14 and is now the assistant scout master

116
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
For their venture patrol

117
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,000
And Walter is in Palo Alto, California

118
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:24,000
Howdy, Walter

119
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Hi there

120
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Larry, we said that you were a scout master

121
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
For several different troops

122
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
If we put all those years together

123
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,000
How many years do you figure you spent as a scout master?

124
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:35,000
I think it's 14

125
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:36,000
Tom, same question

126
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:37,000
18

127
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,000
18 years

128
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:39,000
And Walter?

129
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
It's about six though I started up with Cubs

130
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
So I've worked my way up through assistant and leader all the way

131
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
Were you involved in Cubs, Tom?

132
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
No, we didn't actually have very many Cubs

133
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
When I was growing up we didn't

134
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:52,000
But my kids were

135
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
And I guess I did the weeblows thing

136
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,000
With the boys before we went into Boy Scouts

137
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:00,000
When they started camping

138
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:01,000
That's what got you interested

139
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,000
Yeah, that's it

140
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:03,000
How about you, Larry?

141
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Have any involvement with Cubs?

142
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
I was the weeblows leader for

143
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
A one year from my eldest son

144
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
You guys all have the experience of having worked in Cubs scouting

145
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Making the transition up to Boy Scouts

146
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:18,000
What kind of person makes a good scout master?

147
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
And I'll turn to you first, Larry

148
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:26,000
I think a lot of scout masters are kind of in a middle position somewhere

149
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:32,000
They're not usually CEOs or guys that are really, really busy

150
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,000
It's usually somebody that understands leadership

151
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:37,000
And can do some of that

152
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,000
But it's not necessarily a business owner or somebody that's so busy

153
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
They don't have time for it

154
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:49,000
And then it's somebody that loves working with Scouts

155
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,000
That wants to really get in there either with their own son

156
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
Or if they don't have a son

157
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
Somebody that really wants to work with them

158
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:57,000
And to learn and to grow

159
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
What would you add to that time?

160
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:05,000
Well, the person would have to be willing to try different things

161
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
To go a little bit outside his comfort zone

162
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
Get out of the box type stuff

163
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
And Walter, what would you add?

164
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
I think especially coming over from Cubs

165
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
You need someone who has the patience to watch boys

166
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Fail as they learn

167
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:23,000
If you can watch boys try to light a fire for 30 minutes

168
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Without saying anything

169
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:32,000
And the other thing is you have to be really solid in the ideals

170
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
The oath and law and what you're about

171
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
And that it's citizenship, character and fitness

172
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
Because you end up explaining this all the time

173
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
It's not the same as soccer teams

174
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
And especially with parents

175
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
You need to be very solid when somebody comes in

176
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
And says, why isn't my son a patrol leader?

177
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Because this is how we do it

178
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,000
So patience, are you as patient now as you were

179
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
When you started out as a scout master, Walter?

180
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
I have learned the scout master position

181
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:03,000
Which is one hand in your pocket

182
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
And the other holding a cup of coffee

183
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
How about you, Larry?

184
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
I think I'm way more patient than I was when I started

185
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Less flappable, more unflappable

186
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:22,000
And all three of you had sons involved

187
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:25,000
What are we looking out for when we take on that position

188
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
And our sons involved?

189
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
I don't want to show favoritism to my own son

190
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,000
So there's a whole lot of let one of the other assistants

191
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
Take care of stuff that happens with your son

192
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
And you'll take care of his son

193
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Larry, what was your experience?

194
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
My guys and I don't think had a problem with that

195
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:46,000
I've asked them about that

196
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
They're both fairly independent

197
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
And they both had good friends in the troop

198
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:51,000
That they hung out with

199
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
I did a few of their scout master conferences

200
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Some other assistant scout masters did them

201
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
I think it worked out pretty well

202
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
Walter, what was your experience?

203
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
When I was asked to be scout master

204
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
I asked my son about it

205
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
And he thought it was a terrible idea

206
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,000
He came around

207
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
And in fact, he was SPL

208
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:14,000
And I was scout master

209
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,000
And that worked out pretty well

210
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
But it was really nice to have

211
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Hand that over to another scout master

212
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:20,000
And he was still SPL

213
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
And let me be able to see them work together

214
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
So patients, having a little bit of

215
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:29,000
You end up having those things that you do as a father

216
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
And those things that you do as a scout master

217
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
And that can be kind of fuzzy at times

218
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
It can be kind of hard to figure

219
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,000
What challenges do you expect a new scout master

220
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Is going to meet?

221
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
Sometimes trying to do

222
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
I can do it better myself

223
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Or it won't get done right if I don't do it

224
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Mentality

225
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
And you've got to realize that

226
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
It will get done

227
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
It won't be the way you wanted it to

228
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:55,000
But it will get done

229
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,000
If you ask

230
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
And everybody's behind the program

231
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,000
So you've got to be careful with that

232
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
I'm a delegator

233
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
And I don't have a lot of problem with that

234
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
When I give something to somebody

235
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,000
You know

236
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
Before long as people come around

237
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:13,000
I give it all away

238
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
The truth is I just don't have much to do

239
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
But for some reason I don't have a lot of anxiety

240
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
Like some guys do

241
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:21,000
About that

242
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
I was a scout from

243
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
My 11th birthday

244
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:26,000
The day of

245
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
Until I was 18

246
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,000
So I had kind of a background

247
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
I did, I thought

248
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
I think for me

249
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
Working with the scouts

250
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
Wasn't as much as

251
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
Working with the parents, the moms

252
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:42,000
And the committee

253
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
That opened up more of the challenges sometimes

254
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,000
I want to get back to that

255
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
Larry, you were fairly experienced in scouting

256
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:49,000
You had some background

257
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Tom, how about you?

258
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
I joined when I was 11

259
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
And eagled out

260
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:55,000
At 18

261
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
I was one of those late eagles

262
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
But I went all the way through the program also

263
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:02,000
And Walter?

264
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
I was a cub scout

265
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
I was a scout through 18

266
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,000
I was on summer camp staff

267
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
One summer when I was in college

268
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
So I had plenty of experience

269
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,000
I thought

270
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
Do you think that somebody without any background in it

271
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
Can actually take the job on?

272
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
The guy that preceded me

273
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Was a retired navy guy

274
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
He was never a scout

275
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:27,000
And he had daughters

276
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
And he was scout master for three years

277
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
He did very well

278
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
And he was a very good scout master

279
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
And really loved the kids

280
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
So yeah, I absolutely think that

281
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
That if it's the right person, they can do that

282
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:44,000
I think that with the network

283
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:47,000
That there is something like this

284
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
With the internet, with roundtables

285
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
With the training

286
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Yes, you could do it

287
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
You could be a good scout master

288
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
With no background in the program

289
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
The two scout masters that followed me

290
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:01,000
Have been both

291
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
Either one had a scouting experience growing up

292
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
And so you certainly have to help them along

293
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:07,000
As scout master emeritus

294
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
And give them hints here and there

295
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:10,000
We're also lucky

296
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,000
Our boys are very, very particular

297
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
About leading the operation

298
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:16,000
And if you try to step in

299
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:17,000
They will let you know

300
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
That it is a boy led troop

301
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:20,000
And you're doing it wrong

302
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
And so even if you don't have

303
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
A whole background of

304
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
Scouting experience as a youth

305
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:27,000
That you can still do this

306
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
If you go and take advantage

307
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
Of the different support networks

308
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:32,000
That are available

309
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
If you kind of listen to people

310
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
Who've got a little bit of experience

311
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
Larry, you started talking a little bit

312
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
About working with parents

313
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
Working with other volunteers

314
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
I think that might

315
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:46,000
I'm not saying that

316
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
You saying that would put people off

317
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
But I think that would be a pretty big factor

318
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:51,000
In somebody who's thinking about

319
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:52,000
Taking the job on

320
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
Is whether or not they could stand doing that

321
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:55,000
I don't know

322
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
I don't think I would have done very well

323
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
If I hadn't had very, very strong committees

324
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
Both times I've been a scout master

325
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:03,000
I've had basically

326
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
The whole time rooms full of people

327
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
From this troop

328
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
We average about 15

329
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:10,000
In my last troop

330
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
We average 20 to 35

331
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:13,000
In a committee meeting

332
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
And with all of those people

333
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:16,000
In the committee

334
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
They get to know

335
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Kind of what's going on

336
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
And don't freak out about

337
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:22,000
Quite as much

338
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,000
As people that are totally on the sideline

339
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
Particularly moms of single

340
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:28,000
Single moms

341
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
They're not in committee meetings

342
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:31,000
They're busy

343
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
It's just harder sometimes, I think, for them

344
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
I have a letter from a mom

345
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
It's about me

346
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Inflexible and insufferable

347
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,000
And I don't know

348
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:41,000
A whole bunch of ins

349
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
And unfortunately my committee chairman

350
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
Had a sense of humor

351
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:46,000
And I don't want to

352
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:47,000
I don't want to be negative about that

353
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:48,000
It's just

354
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
It can be a challenge on occasions

355
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:51,000
Working through that

356
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
Like you said, when parents don't understand

357
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
Why their son's not patrol leader

358
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
You know, you got to walk through that

359
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
We're talking to somebody

360
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
Who maybe is considering being a scoutmaster

361
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
Who's a newly minted scoutmaster

362
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
And they're encountering some of these things

363
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:08,000
And you do get through them, don't you?

364
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:09,000
Yeah

365
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
Oh, absolutely

366
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
I would agree that a committee chair

367
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:14,000
Is really essential

368
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:15,000
And we have

369
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:16,000
Our committee meetings

370
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:17,000
Really a parent meeting

371
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:18,000
We have them

372
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
During the first troop meeting of the month

373
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,000
Right

374
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
And so the parents are all over in another room

375
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
The scoutmaster's typically there

376
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
Letting the SPL run the meeting

377
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
And off we'll have the SPL come in

378
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
And explain something that's going on

379
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:32,000
But that really helps

380
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
I actually spent more effort

381
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
Rebuilding the connection

382
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
With our charter organization

383
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
You know, kind of getting

384
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
Reconnected with the pastor

385
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
Having the whole troop show up

386
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
For parish workdays

387
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
In troop t-shirts

388
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,000
And really just getting that back

389
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:53,000
On track

390
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
And I want to talk more about that

391
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:58,000
But just talking about overcoming

392
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
Those first couple of months of resistance

393
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:03,000
Or difficulty, you know

394
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
Being the new guy in the job

395
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
Does anybody have anything

396
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
That they specifically remember from that time?

397
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
The way I look at it

398
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
My committee chairman and I

399
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
Is that the committee chairman and I

400
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
Are in charge and responsible for everything

401
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
Anything in the troop that doesn't happen

402
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
There's not somebody to cover

403
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:23,000
We do

404
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
And so the idea is

405
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Is that we don't push people

406
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
To do stuff, even the scouts

407
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
We say what we want is everybody to do

408
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
What they're trained, experienced

409
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:36,000
And comfortable doing

410
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
And if you work it that way

411
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
Eventually it takes time sometimes

412
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
But eventually you'll find a guy

413
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
That wants to be treasurer for eight years

414
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
And he never loses a penny

415
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
Because he's doing what he wants to do

416
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
If you'd made him an advancement chair

417
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:51,000
Or a committee chair

418
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
You'd have had a disaster

419
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
So you just keep working through

420
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
And the same thing with the scouts

421
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
Some guys don't want to be SPL

422
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:00,000
They want to be instructors

423
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:01,000
Some guys, you know

424
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:02,000
It just depends

425
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:03,000
You have to work with them

426
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
And find out what exactly

427
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,000
Where they fit

428
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:07,000
And then encourage them

429
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:08,000
To go do that

430
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:09,000
And do it well

431
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:10,000
Tom, do you remember

432
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
That first period after you decided

433
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
That you would go ahead and take on the job

434
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:15,000
Was it really challenging?

435
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:16,000
It was a little bit like

436
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:17,000
What Larry was talking about

437
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
Since we were a brand new troop

438
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:20,000
We didn't have many adults

439
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:23,000
And I was doing a lot of the stuff

440
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:26,000
But as we needed stuff

441
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
Even if there weren't a position

442
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
Even if nobody volunteered for anything

443
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:35,000
You could always go and ask a parent

444
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:39,000
And if it was a close-ended thing

445
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
They would say, sure, I'll do that

446
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:44,000
And sort of got those things off of me

447
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
Back into the committee

448
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
Or then started getting the boys

449
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
To doing the stuff

450
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:54,000
So it started shifting back to where it should be

451
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:58,000
And leaving me and the committee chair

452
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
Doing what we're supposed to be doing

453
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,000
And Walter, you talked about having to

454
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
Kind of rebuild the chartered

455
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
Organization relationship

456
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
As one of the challenges

457
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:12,000
That come easily to mind?

458
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:16,000
Well, when my son joined

459
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:17,000
We had a big year

460
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:21,000
And the troop grew from 23 to 46 scouts

461
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
And then I became

462
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
Scoutmaster that March

463
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:28,000
So we were really adjusting

464
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:31,000
To being a more organized

465
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
Little bit less seat of the pants

466
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:34,000
Troop

467
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
And we had really excellent

468
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
Outings chair moved on

469
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:39,000
That was a real adjustment

470
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
To just get more people

471
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
Stepping in to do more things

472
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
Because we were bigger

473
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:48,000
And get the scouts used to doing things

474
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
As opposed to just a little group

475
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:53,000
To knew each other as to a bigger group

476
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:54,000
So that was...

477
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
There was a real adjustment there

478
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
And I was shepherding a lot of that

479
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
So Walter, when you...

480
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
When your outdoor guy left

481
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:03,000
Did you and the committee chair

482
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
Pick that up until, like say

483
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
For six months until the right guy

484
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:08,000
Came along?

485
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
We did have somebody volunteer

486
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:11,000
And they were pretty good

487
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
But they weren't nearly as good

488
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:16,000
As the previous guy

489
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,000
But did you get another one eventually?

490
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:20,000
Yes, yes

491
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
We're a most recent outdoor chair

492
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
We've gone to be Scoutmaster

493
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:25,000
And now we have another one

494
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:28,000
And so that's really settled down

495
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
Now that we've adjusted to the size we are

496
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
And of course now we just got another 20 scouts

497
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:35,000
So we have to have another adjustment

498
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
Well, we've talked a lot about

499
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
Importance of a strong troop committee

500
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:40,000
We've talked a little bit about

501
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
Relating to the charter organization

502
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:45,000
Larry, what's your experience

503
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
As far as the importance of

504
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
Having good connections with the charter organization?

505
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:52,000
I was in troop 705 for about two years

506
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:53,000
And it wasn't going really well

507
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:54,000
So we started a new one

508
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:57,000
And both my pastors were Eagle Scouts

509
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
The current one and then the previous one

510
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
And were extremely supportive

511
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
It was an excellent relationship

512
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:04,000
How about you, Tom?

513
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:07,000
They're very standoffish

514
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:10,000
But they are in the charter organization

515
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:14,000
He's had three sons

516
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:15,000
Two of them have become Eagles

517
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
The third one is working very hard

518
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
And he's in the troop

519
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:22,000
And he's in the charter organization

520
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,000
So he is our link

521
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:28,000
And one of the other key relationships

522
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
Is the relationship to the district

523
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:31,000
And the council

524
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
What are your thoughts on that, fellas?

525
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
This will probably get censored

526
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
I encourage people to take training

527
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
But particularly in my first troop

528
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
I kept most of my guys fairly isolated

529
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
I went to Roundtable

530
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:49,000
And the rest of the district at the time

531
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
Was the kind of thing where

532
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:54,000
If I brought my committee data and nabbed them

533
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:55,000
When you say that

534
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:56,000
You mean they would have asked them

535
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
To volunteer and do something

536
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:01,000
And we ended up along the way

537
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
With 50, 60, 70 scouts

538
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:06,000
And I sent the assistant scout masters to training

539
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
And the main thing

540
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
That we had a relationship with the district

541
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
Was the district advancement chair

542
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
But you have to have a good relationship

543
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
With the district advancement chair

544
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:17,000
Particularly if you're going to have

545
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:18,000
Eagle scouts

546
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:19,000
That's essential

547
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:20,000
I don't know

548
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:21,000
It depends on your troop

549
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
We have troops that have trailers

550
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
And they have all kinds of gear

551
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:26,000
And the biggest thing of the year

552
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
Is the two camperees

553
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
And they set all this stuff up

554
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
And we're the guys that

555
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:33,000
You know, that park at the gate

556
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:34,000
And backpack in

557
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
And go back in the bushes

558
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:39,000
That's sort of us too

559
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
We're a very minimalist backpacking troop

560
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
We don't have a trailer

561
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
The district and the Roundtables

562
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
I think are pretty invaluable

563
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
Because you do gain an awful lot

564
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:52,000
Of knowledge

565
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:55,000
The other scout masters

566
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
The other committee district people

567
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:58,000
That are there

568
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
Are more than willing to share their experiences

569
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
And you find out

570
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
Hey, you really aren't in this by yourself

571
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
In the district

572
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
And I was the

573
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
District advancement chair

574
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
For a couple of years, I guess

575
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:13,000
A few years ago

576
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,000
And that's a whole another

577
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:16,000
Being on the district side

578
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:19,000
Is a whole another story

579
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,000
I think the district is

580
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
Being associated in the district

581
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:25,000
Is very useful

582
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:26,000
Yes, I would agree with that

583
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
I know I'm about to find out

584
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:29,000
About the other side

585
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
I've just been invited to be on

586
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
To be on the district advancement committee

587
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
And help approve eagle projects

588
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
But no, our district is

589
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
Very lightweight and helpful

590
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
Our troop is also a backpacking troop

591
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
As are most of the troops around here

592
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:46,000
Like Tom, a lot of what

593
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:47,000
I get out of it

594
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:49,000
Is knowing the other scout masters

595
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:52,000
Right, there's a real comradery

596
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:53,000
Among the troops

597
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:54,000
When I was a kid

598
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
It seemed like there was a rivalry

599
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
Between the troops

600
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
But nowadays it seems like

601
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:03,000
Especially being an adult now

602
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:05,000
Everybody really tries to get along

603
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,000
With each other because

604
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:09,000
You're doing the same thing

605
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:11,000
Now, one thing I'll mention that

606
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:13,000
Is we live in Florida

607
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,000
And it's hot and it's buggy

608
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,000
So we go to Camp Daniel Boone

609
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:18,000
And Camp North Carolina

610
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
Which is the Asheville council

611
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:21,000
It's in the mountains

612
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:22,000
It's 3,500 feet

613
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
It's cool, it's beautiful

614
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
Again, that sets us off

615
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:29,000
If you tend to go to your local camp

616
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
You also make connections

617
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:32,000
That we don't make

618
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
True, more than just your committee

619
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
And the other people that are working with your troop

620
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
You can stretch out a little bit

621
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
You can find some help, assistance

622
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
Some comradery with people

623
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
In your district and your council as well

624
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
Right, the council

625
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:49,000
The person you want to really know

626
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:50,000
In the council level

627
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:51,000
When the council level

628
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,000
Is the secretary for camping

629
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,000
And the secretary for advancement

630
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
Those are the two

631
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,000
Yeah, they know how to get things done

632
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:00,000
Absolutely

633
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
Let's talk a little bit about

634
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
Working with your youth leaders

635
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
There's different styles of doing this

636
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
There's different ways of approaching it

637
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
What's the dynamic

638
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
Of the youth leadership

639
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
So far as your role as Scoutmaster is involved

640
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:17,000
If you focus on the leaders

641
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:21,000
And you kind of leave the other guys alone

642
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:23,000
You begin to develop that relationship

643
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:25,000
Where the older guys

644
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:27,000
Take care of the younger guys

645
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:28,000
Everybody's different there

646
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:29,000
I guess is what I would say

647
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,000
Is I've seen different Scoutmasters

648
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:33,000
Different ways

649
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:35,000
And I'm pretty much hands off

650
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:36,000
Most of the time

651
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:39,000
But some people are more hands on

652
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,000
And it seems to work for them

653
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
And the boys still do the thing

654
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:44,000
They still do scouting

655
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
So how you work that out

656
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
And getting to know the guys

657
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:49,000
When you're brand new

658
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,000
Is just something that you have to do

659
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:53,000
As a Scoutmaster

660
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:55,000
And it's the

661
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:56,000
Especially the senior patrol leader

662
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
Depends on how old he is

663
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:01,000
How much experience he might have

664
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:05,000
And if he's a go-getter

665
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
You just step back and let him go

666
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,000
If he's very timid

667
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:11,000
You have to say

668
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:13,000
You have to send him notes through emails

669
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:15,000
The week before

670
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:17,000
And let him cultivate that

671
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:19,000
For the next meeting

672
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:23,000
So the age and maturity

673
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:24,000
Of your senior patrol leader

674
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:27,000
Is going to affect how you handle it

675
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:31,000
No matter whether you're new or old

676
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:33,000
Right, but we're talking about new guys here

677
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:34,000
Right

678
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
I guess that's what we're going to focus on

679
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:37,000
And you don't know them

680
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:38,000
Well, you may not

681
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:39,000
I don't know

682
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:40,000
If you've been in the troop a while

683
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:41,000
But if you walk in

684
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:42,000
And you don't know them

685
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:43,000
You have to develop that relationship

686
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,000
Well, that's true too, yeah

687
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:48,000
So that's hard coming in, cold

688
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:51,000
It is a real delicate balance

689
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:55,000
Between how much do you let them

690
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,000
Risk and fail

691
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
While you're still responsible

692
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,000
For the program of the troop

693
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:03,000
So there's a real balance

694
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:06,000
Of that sort of mentoring

695
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
While having the campouts happen

696
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
And having things happen on them

697
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:11,000
And advancement

698
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:12,000
And it's just hard

699
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:13,000
Right

700
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:15,000
It's one of those subtle balance

701
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:16,000
Sorts of things

702
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:19,000
That you just have to work out

703
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:20,000
From my personal experience

704
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
It takes a while to figure out

705
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:23,000
Exactly how it all works

706
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:24,000
Whether you've had any experience

707
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:25,000
In scouting or not

708
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:28,000
It takes a lot of practice

709
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
To find all the subtleties

710
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:34,000
Of working and motivating youth leaders

711
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:37,000
Well, it depends on how much of it

712
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:40,000
Is leading and motivating

713
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:42,000
And how much of it is them finding

714
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:43,000
Their own way

715
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:44,000
It's a little bit of both

716
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:47,000
Because you're letting them go

717
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:49,000
To figure out their own leadership ability

718
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:51,000
Some of them, you know

719
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:53,000
You can't teach every kid

720
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:55,000
Because every kid is different

721
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:56,000
You teach them some principles

722
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:59,000
But then they have to go and do that

723
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:01,000
Some kids are just loud

724
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:02,000
And a little bit bossy

725
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:03,000
But it works for them

726
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:05,000
You know, another kid try that

727
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:06,000
And you know, it's just like

728
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:07,000
Who are you?

729
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:08,000
And then other guys

730
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:09,000
Are just kind of quiet

731
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:11,000
And they're more in the background

732
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:12,000
And yet guys just walk over

733
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
And say, hey, what do you think we should do?

734
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
You know, so their leadership styles

735
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,000
Can be very, very different

736
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:19,000
And they have to kind of

737
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:21,000
The scouts have to kind of find that

738
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:24,000
And that's where you encourage them

739
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:25,000
In their leadership

740
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:27,000
But you don't drown it out

741
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:29,000
Hey, you know, you're not doing

742
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:30,000
You know, senior patrol

743
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:31,000
You're not doing enough of this

744
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:33,000
You know, or that kind of thing

745
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:36,000
As opposed to, well, you really handle that well

746
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:40,000
How can you take whatever you did there

747
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:43,000
And use it over here in this situation

748
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:45,000
And they have to figure that out

749
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:47,000
You can't say, well, then you need to do that

750
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:49,000
Because that won't work

751
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:51,000
Is there anything that you did

752
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:53,000
Any of you did particularly

753
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:54,000
That you would look back on and you say

754
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
I would really, I would warn somebody

755
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:57,000
Not to do that

756
00:28:57,000 --> 00:28:58,000
Had a great experience

757
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
Part of our part of growing

758
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,000
Was that we split into more patrols

759
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
And so we had the leaders

760
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:06,000
Scouts do that

761
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:08,000
And they did a fantastic job

762
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:09,000
And then one of the

763
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:10,000
We have assistive patrol leaders

764
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
Associated with the patrol

765
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:13,000
So they're kind of liaisons

766
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:15,000
And so someone had been with one of these patrols

767
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:17,000
For a long time

768
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:18,000
Looked at the result and said

769
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:20,000
Well, some of the scouts I've been with

770
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:22,000
For all these years are now in another patrol

771
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,000
And could we change all this

772
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,000
And I let him change

773
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:28,000
And that was a mistake

774
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
Because we, that haunted us

775
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:31,000
For a couple of years

776
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:32,000
Because they had it arranged

777
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,000
So they were kind of younger

778
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,000
Kind of a bench for leadership

779
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,000
So when the patrol leader moved on

780
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:39,000
And so we ended up with one patrol

781
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:41,000
That didn't have anybody to fill in

782
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:42,000
For the next patrol leader

783
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:44,000
Another patrol with too many older scouts

784
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:45,000
That all aged out at once

785
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:47,000
And boy, yeah, I let the adults

786
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:49,000
Mess with the boys' decisions

787
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:50,000
That was a bad idea

788
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:52,000
So Walter, what you're describing is

789
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:54,000
The boys set up their own patrols

790
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:56,000
And then you allowed

791
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,000
The adults to kind of overrule that

792
00:29:58,000 --> 00:29:59,000
They just made some adjustments

793
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:00,000
Yeah

794
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:02,000
It was very finely balanced work

795
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:04,000
Boys did a fantastic job

796
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:05,000
Yeah

797
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:06,000
And it threw it out of balance

798
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:08,000
Hmm, that's interesting

799
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:10,000
The word I would use is meddling

800
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:12,000
And I think that's

801
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:14,000
One of the regrets I would have

802
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:16,000
Is I have meddled a couple of times

803
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:18,000
In SPL elections

804
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:20,000
And patrol organization

805
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:22,000
Once or twice

806
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:24,000
Shouldn't have

807
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:26,000
We started to mess around with

808
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:28,000
The patrol arrangement once

809
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:30,000
And backed off

810
00:30:30,000 --> 00:30:32,000
I don't remember exactly why

811
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:34,000
But we backed off of that

812
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:36,000
But we meddled around

813
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:38,000
Trying to do troop rules

814
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:40,000
You know

815
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:42,000
And the troop rules are

816
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:44,000
Her in the book

817
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,000
So, you know

818
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,000
And what rules are you talking about specifically?

819
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:50,000
We were trying to get

820
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:52,000
Punishment rules

821
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:54,000
If you do this, then this will

822
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:55,000
Happen

823
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:57,000
And we had a trouble

824
00:30:57,000 --> 00:30:58,000
Trouble child

825
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:00,000
And we were trying to lay down more rules

826
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:02,000
To cover what he was doing

827
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:04,000
Instead of going

828
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:06,000
You're not living up to the oath and the law

829
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:07,000
These are the rules

830
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:09,000
So that

831
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:11,000
That came about and lasted

832
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:13,000
For a very short time

833
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:15,000
And it's

834
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:17,000
It's gone away now

835
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:19,000
So meddling

836
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:21,000
Like thinking that you're smarter than the boys

837
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:23,000
Because a lot of times we're not

838
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:25,000
Well, I don't know

839
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:27,000
That it's that we're smarter

840
00:31:27,000 --> 00:31:28,000
Sometimes we are smarter

841
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:30,000
Sometimes we could do it better

842
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:32,000
But when we meddle

843
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:34,000
We take away that learning process

844
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,000
Whether they would do it better, the same or less

845
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:38,000
It doesn't matter

846
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:40,000
The point is it's not

847
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:42,000
Whether we're smarter or whether there's a better end result

848
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:44,000
The point is that they did it

849
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:47,000
And they learned one way or the other from that

850
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:49,000
And that's the main

851
00:31:49,000 --> 00:31:51,000
The other thing that came up was

852
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:53,000
Overlaying a whole lot of other things

853
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:55,000
Of the program in the way of rules

854
00:31:55,000 --> 00:31:57,000
And regulations and stuff like that

855
00:31:57,000 --> 00:31:59,000
I've made that mistake

856
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:01,000
My hands up

857
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:03,000
I definitely did that in the past

858
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:05,000
And found out that

859
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:07,000
If most of those rules and regulations

860
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:09,000
Had any merit to them

861
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:11,000
They would have been there in the first place

862
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:14,000
Can't go wrong if you stick to the program

863
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:16,000
So we've talked a lot about

864
00:32:16,000 --> 00:32:18,000
The different challenges that you come across

865
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:20,000
Some of the relationships that you need to think about

866
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:22,000
The way that you work with the scouts

867
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:24,000
How about at home? How about dealing with

868
00:32:24,000 --> 00:32:26,000
Your family, your wife and

869
00:32:26,000 --> 00:32:28,000
How that all works?

870
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:30,000
I mean everybody knows

871
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:32,000
I go to work and I'm a scout

872
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:35,000
My wife and boys have always supported that

873
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:37,000
I'm a scout guy

874
00:32:37,000 --> 00:32:39,000
Everybody knows that

875
00:32:39,000 --> 00:32:41,000
Everybody that knows me is the scout guy

876
00:32:41,000 --> 00:32:43,000
I guess if you stick with it for a while

877
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:45,000
That's going to get attached to you

878
00:32:45,000 --> 00:32:47,000
That's associated with you

879
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:49,000
And with your wife

880
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:51,000
So it's a family thing

881
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:53,000
Completely

882
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:55,000
You do become

883
00:32:55,000 --> 00:32:57,000
The program

884
00:32:57,000 --> 00:32:59,000
People do recognize you

885
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:01,000
Because of stuff you've done through the scouting

886
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:03,000
And

887
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:05,000
Your wife

888
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:07,000
Does become the wife

889
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:09,000
Of the scout master

890
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:11,000
Whether they know her or not

891
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:13,000
They know that she's the wife of the scout master

892
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:15,000
But she's got to support it

893
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:17,000
And

894
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:19,000
My boys

895
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:21,000
You lost your ticket

896
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:23,000
But you still get to go on the ride

897
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,000
Absolutely

898
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:27,000
You're almost as old as the rest of us

899
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:29,000
Walter, what do you think about that?

900
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:31,000
I'm a preacher's kid

901
00:33:31,000 --> 00:33:33,000
And my mom

902
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:35,000
Always made it very clear

903
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:37,000
That she was not the preacher's wife

904
00:33:37,000 --> 00:33:39,000
She was Merle's wife

905
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:41,000
And that was her number one

906
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:43,000
As opposed to being just another

907
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:45,000
Hand around the corner

908
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:47,000
And that was her number one

909
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:49,000
As opposed to being just another hand

910
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:51,000
Around the church

911
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:53,000
And so I tried to make sure that

912
00:33:53,000 --> 00:33:55,000
It's really my thing

913
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:57,000
And it's really

914
00:33:57,000 --> 00:33:59,000
I get fantastic support from the family

915
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:01,000
But that they're first

916
00:34:01,000 --> 00:34:03,000
Now

917
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:05,000
Tina thought it was a little weird at first

918
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,000
With all these little kids running around in blue uniforms

919
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:09,000
And it is a little weird

920
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:11,000
But they

921
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:13,000
She's actually came around and volunteered

922
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:15,000
And organized the boards of review

923
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:17,000
For the past several years

924
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:19,000
And has really enjoyed

925
00:34:19,000 --> 00:34:21,000
Talking to the scouts and the boards

926
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:23,000
So it's been great support

927
00:34:23,000 --> 00:34:25,000
But it's really important

928
00:34:25,000 --> 00:34:27,000
To keep that

929
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:29,000
In balance

930
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:31,000
So this group has a number of years

931
00:34:31,000 --> 00:34:33,000
Behind them as being a volunteer

932
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:35,000
Leader in scouting

933
00:34:35,000 --> 00:34:37,000
And it's a lot

934
00:34:37,000 --> 00:34:39,000
It's a lot to ask of somebody

935
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:41,000
It makes

936
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:43,000
There's challenges and demands on your time

937
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:45,000
Any regrets?

938
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:47,000
Would you recommend it to folks?

939
00:34:47,000 --> 00:34:49,000
Absolutely

940
00:34:49,000 --> 00:34:51,000
No regrets

941
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:53,000
No regrets

942
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:55,000
I would encourage people to make sure that

943
00:34:55,000 --> 00:34:57,000
You find some enthusiasm in it

944
00:34:57,000 --> 00:34:59,000
If you like to hike

945
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:01,000
Make sure that there's

946
00:35:01,000 --> 00:35:03,000
That you're really having fun doing

947
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:05,000
Yeah I agree

948
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:07,000
No regrets at all

949
00:35:07,000 --> 00:35:09,000
It's the greatest thing

950
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:11,000
Well you're the scout guy

951
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:13,000
I know

952
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:15,000
Well listen

953
00:35:15,000 --> 00:35:17,000
I really appreciate everybody joining in

954
00:35:17,000 --> 00:35:19,000
I hope folks will find this

955
00:35:19,000 --> 00:35:21,000
Valuable

956
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:23,000
If you would like us to talk about

957
00:35:23,000 --> 00:35:25,000
Another subject in scouting

958
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:27,000
Or if you're having a particular

959
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:29,000
Problem or challenge

960
00:35:29,000 --> 00:35:31,000
Get in touch

961
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:33,000
And we'll do it again

962
